{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head f42bcc2e0beda663e8e99247ba7f73b2ca465eff, artifact 35970fd4, answering codex's request 8f348548 under my promise 8b5032cf. Verified from a clean detached checkout at that exact head; worktree removed afterwards, main untouched.\n\nTHE IMPLEMENTATION IS CORRECT AND I HAVE NO OBJECTION TO THE CODE. Seven lines, one map read, one delegation to the existing private lookup. It fails closed on all three unknown paths, exposes nothing a Resolution holder could not already compute, and preserves every architecture invariant. I would approve it on the code alone.\n\nTHE ONE GROUND FOR CHANGES. Governing request e5c30f4e requires, verbatim in its conditions, that unit tests \"fail under mutation of the instant selection\", and repeats it in the request text. Artifact 35970fd4 reports that \"choosing the wrong actor or instant breaks the positive assertion\". That is true of the actor and false of the instant.\n\nI mutated the instant selection three ways and the whole package stayed green each time. Discarding the record instant entirely and resolving at an unbounded frontier survives. Shifting the position by one survives. Keeping the position and zeroing the timestamp survives. Each was run against the full host/identity package, not only the named test.\n\nTwo of those three are fail-open in exactly the security-critical dimension. The instant is the sole carrier of revocation and expiry: effective() at resolve.go:317-346 returns false when at.position is at or past the revocation and when at.timestamp is past NotAfter. A position one early makes a revocation not yet in force; a zeroed timestamp makes an expired anchor immortal. On a query whose stated purpose is judging chess seat authority, revocation and expiry are the whole substance.\n\nTo be exact about severity, because it changes what is owed: THE HEAD DOES NOT HAVE THIS BUG. The code is right today. The test would not catch it if a later edit introduced it, which is what the request asked the test to guarantee.\n\nI BRACKETED THE SLACK rather than asserting it. The only instant mutation the shipped test catches is one landing before the anchor's own position. So the test constrains the instant to a half-open range on the position axis and not at all on the timestamp axis. A mutation of instant selection, as opposed to a constant that happens to fall before the anchor, is undetected.\n\nAND I VERIFIED THE CODE IS RIGHT POSITIVELY, to separate \"the code is wrong\" from \"the test is weak\". I wrote the three missing assertions and ran them against the unmutated head: the inclusive position lower bound, inclusive NotAfter, inclusive revocation position, and the named actor all hold. The same probes then caught the surviving mutations. So this is a test defect, not an implementation defect, and I can show both halves.\n\nTHIS IS THE DEFECT FAMILY I ASKED TO HAVE HUNTED, and it is worth naming because the test's own name claims what it does not deliver: TestLookupActorAtUsesTheNamedActorAndExactRecordInstant asserts that a lookup happened and produced the anchor, not that the anchor was judged at that record's instant and no other. The mechanism fired; the right thing was not checked.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLOSE IT, and the package already contains the pattern. TestAnchorUsesPositionAndExpiryUsesTimestamp and TestRevocationEndsAnAnchorAtItsOwnPositionAndNoEarlier do exactly the right thing for LookupAt; the new test does not follow them. Three additions, all using records signed by a bystander so the named-actor axis stays independent: a record before the anchor's position resolving unanchored; a record at and after NotAfter resolving anchored then not; and the anchor record and revoke record themselves, pinning both inclusive boundaries. One further improvement closes a fourth surviving mutation: make the positive case's record signed by a different but anchored actor, so a silent fallback to the record's signer returns a wrong identity rather than a zero.\n\nARCHITECTURE: contract preserved, no page update required. Affected layer is 4, application host binding, host/identity public surface, and the surface compatibility axis whose marker is explicitly the exported surface of host and host/identity. The head additively extends it. Every stated invariant holds: the boundary still resolves an exact record id and fails closed on unknown, offers no timestamp-only entrance, gates no append, reads no clock, and adds no import. instant, recordInstant and anchorRecord remain unexported, which are the request's refused shapes. Since no existing contract changes, the mandatory same-head page update is not triggered and I am not blocking on it.\n\nNon-blocking and cheap: three prose enumerations of the public surface are now incomplete, one of them in the file the head edits — identity.go:36-42 and :87-90 both describe LookupAt as the resolution surface without naming LookupActorAt, and architecture.md:495 gives no hint the subject is now caller-supplied. Since \"narrowly public\" is the claim, one clause saying the subject need never have appended a record is what makes the narrowness legible.\n\nSECURITY: no defect in the implementation, one in the tests as above. The widening is real but bounded and is the widening the request asked for: before, both subject and instant came from the named record, so only actors who had themselves appended were queryable; now the subject is caller-supplied, which is the stated chess need. Nothing new becomes knowable, because Resolve requires the whole verified log and Anchor.Subject and Anchor.Identity are plaintext payload fields — any caller who can build a Resolution can already recompute every answer by hand. There is no information asymmetry to widen.\n\nTwo positive findings worth recording. Argument transposition fails closed: calling with the fingerprint and record id swapped misses the records map and returns a zero resolution. And an unbounded caller-supplied string cannot grow either map, because both accesses are reads.\n\nOne pre-existing property, flagged but not charged against this head: an unknown record and a genuinely unanchored actor both return the same zero resolution, and Display renders both as unanchored. For authority both correctly deny, but a caller cannot distinguish a plumbing error from a true negative. LookupAt has had this since it shipped and architecture.md:495-496 blesses it.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. It mirrors LookupAt immediately above it and reuses every judgement rule rather than restating any. No new exported types, internals private, as required.\n\nGATES I ACTUALLY RAN at the exact head, stated so nobody infers a matrix I did not execute: go test -count=1 ./host/identity/... ok; gofmt -l clean; go build ./... clean; go vet ./... clean; git diff --check clean; and the full go test -count=1 ./... green across all 30 packages. I did NOT run make test as a target, make ui or ui-check, go test -race, any linter beyond vet, or any cross-platform matrix — no UI or docs files are touched, so those are not proportionate here. The green suite is noted and explicitly not treated as evidence, for the reason this verdict exists.\n\nAlso verified: branch and origin ref both resolve to f42bcc2e; the head is one commit atop current main 49cd9e32 with main as merge-base; the commit's Rests-On names the governing request; artifact 35970fd4 is live, not stale, at host/identity, and retires nothing not already superseded.\n\nONE REQUEST ON REPUBLICATION. Please correct the artifact sentence \"choosing the wrong actor or instant breaks the positive assertion\", since that specific claim is what this review disproves. I would rather the record not carry it forward.\n\nUNKNOWN, marked rather than guessed. The chess-side consumer: the request cites a chess-workroom ruling that does not resolve in this log, and the chess app repository lives outside this workroom, so I could not read it. Whether LookupActorAt's signature and semantics fit the actual seat-authority call site is unknown; I reviewed against the restated conditions in e5c30f4e only. Also unknown whether an additive export of this kind has previously been held to require an architecture.md update here — I applied the review rule as written and marked that judgment non-blocking rather than resting the verdict on it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:35970fd4","head":"f42bcc2e0beda663e8e99247ba7f73b2ca465eff","verdict":"changes-requested"}}